# dnv: the controller node agent (architecture.md, Components: invocation
# reference). Run one on a machine: two cn agents on one kernel are never
# safe.
#
# Install as /etc/systemd/system/dnv-agent-cn.service, with the program as
# /usr/local/bin/dnv-agent, the modules of deploy/modules-load.d/dnv-cn.conf,
# the udev rules of deploy/udev/ and its settings as
# /etc/dnv/dnv-agent-cn.yaml, made from deploy/dnv/dnv-agent-cn.yaml. Its
# local store is the default folder, /var/lib/dnv, which this unit names and
# makes. An agent that ran before with a store in another folder finds this
# one empty and starts as after a lost store (cnagent.md CN2): before its
# first start under this unit, with the agent stopped, move its files, those
# whose names start with cn-, cntlr-, clone-bm- or torn-, into /var/lib/dnv.
#
# The kit enables nothing, and advises to enable this unit:
# "systemctl start dnv-agent-cn" starts the agent; "systemctl enable
# dnv-agent-cn" also starts it at boot, once the modules are loaded,
# configfs and debugfs are mounted and the network is up. The agent tells a
# reboot from a restart by the kernel's boot id, so after a reboot the
# worker fails each primary on the node over at the usual time while
# another cntlr of its pool serves, though the agent answers that it is
# starting (dnagent.md SH33, dnv-worker.md HL8); the first start after an
# upgrade, or after the agent's local files were lost, has no saved boot id
# and is held as a restart. The cost of leaving it off: after a reboot the
# agent stays stopped until you start it, each primary on the node fails
# over where its pool has a standby, and once the node's cntlrs have been
# unhealthy for the pool's cntlr_unhealthy, 600 seconds at the default, the
# worker replaces each of them on another controller node (dnv-worker.md
# AR5, AR7).
#
# Order: every worker stops before any agent does, and the workers start
# last, once the agents have started again and their startup passes have
# ended (architecture.md, Upgrade in lockstep; the operations doc's "Upgrade
# dnv"). On a machine that also runs dnv-worker.service,
# "Before=dnv-worker.service" makes systemd stop the worker first when the
# two stop together, as at shutdown. When they start together, as at boot,
# it starts the worker's process after the agent's, not after the agent's
# startup pass: a new worker drives nothing for its first --vote-grace-time
# seconds (dnv-worker.md VW7), and it holds its verdicts on an agent that
# answers that it is starting for up to the start hold (dnv-worker.md HL8).
# Across machines, at an upgrade, and for a unit started or stopped alone,
# you keep the order yourself.
#
# Stop: systemd sends SIGTERM to the agent's main process alone and kills
# nothing else of the unit (KillMode=process); the agent then stops. It
# cancels its background work and waits only for its checks of thin-pool
# metadata, whose thin_check holds a metadata device open (cnagent.md CN13),
# and the "nvme disconnect" its sweep runs in the background may go on after
# it (dnagent.md SH27): inside the kernel's delete of a controller whose
# target is gone, it waits out nvme_core's admin timeout, which no signal
# ends.
# Under KillMode=mixed or control-group, systemd would wait for it before it
# counts the stop as done, so a restart could leave the agent stopped that
# long, longer than a controller node that holds a primary may stay stopped
# (the operations doc's "A planned restart of an agent"). With this mode the
# next start goes ahead beside it, and systemd logs it as a left-over
# process; after a crash, the commands the old agent was running are left to
# end in the same way, a thin_check among them, which the next agent's check
# of the same metadata meets as a busy device and waits out. Send it no
# second signal: a second one ends the agent at once, without its stop.
# Restart=on-failure starts again an agent that
# exited with an error, such as one whose gRPC address this machine does not
# hold yet, or one whose start check refused it, 5 seconds after it exited
# (RestartSec=5s): a wait that keeps an agent which fails again and again
# within systemd's default start limit, 5 starts in 10 seconds, past which
# systemd starts it no more. The cost: the wait is longer than a controller
# node that holds a primary may stay stopped, so after a crash of the agent
# each primary on the node can fail over where its pool has a standby.
#
# Log: the journal. Keep the journal on another filesystem than the store,
# or cap its size (SystemMaxUse= in journald.conf): an agent that cannot
# write its store refuses the worker's syncups, a failover's among them (the
# operations doc's "The logs").
[Unit]
Description=dnv controller node agent
Wants=network-online.target sys-kernel-config.mount sys-kernel-debug.mount
After=network-online.target systemd-modules-load.service sys-kernel-config.mount sys-kernel-debug.mount
Before=dnv-worker.service

[Service]
Type=exec
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/dnv-agent cn --config /etc/dnv/dnv-agent-cn.yaml --local-store /var/lib/dnv
StateDirectory=dnv
KillMode=process
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5s

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
